53515625, -0.01180267333984375, -0.01029205322265625, 0.0139617919921875, -0.01206207275390625, 0.0103302001953125, -0.006595611572265625, -0.00904083251953125, -0.01552581787109375, -0.01009368896484375, -0.02630615234375, 0.00980377197265625, 0.0006909370422363281, 0.0260162353515625, 0.00550079345703125, -0.022857666015625, 0.00647735595703125, 0.01329803466796875, -0.0159454345703125, 0.007534027099609375, 0.0178985595703125, 0.007110595703125, 0.0179290771484375, -0.006710052490234375, 0.049102783203125, -0.0169677734375, 0.006496429443359375, 0.012359619140625, 0.027191162109375, -0.01428985595703125, -0.00539398193359375, 0.020904541015625, -0.038848876953125, -0.0026454925537109375, -0.00460052490234375, -0.0173492431640625, -0.00022983551025390625, 0.0026187896728515625, 0.020050048828125, -0.049560546875, 0.017333984375, -0.0180511474609375, -0.0022792816162109375, -0.01306915283203125, 0.0067291259765625, -0.0144500732421875, -0.003009796142578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Componentwise weak convergence of signed synaptic kernels\u2014meaning that the excitatory and inhibitory synaptic measures converge weakly at each component\u2014does not, by itself, determine the fast-synapse limit of a sparse threshold-reset network. In such networks, the precise temporal order in which excitatory and inhibitory inputs arrive at a neuron can crucially affect whether the neuron fires, even if the aggregate synaptic input converges in distribution. Within a causal event protocol with clamped refractoriness and smooth positive-delay kernels (where each synaptic event has a strictly positive delay and the kernel is smooth), we construct two families of networks whose excitatory and inhibitory measures both converge weakly to the Dirac delta at zero ($\\delta_0$), but with reversed microscopic arrival orders of excitation and inhibition. \n\nThis arrival order matters: in the excitatory-first family, a target neuron fires, while in the inhibitory-first family, it does not, precisely when the initial state $x$ and pulse masses $a$ (excitatory) and $b$ (inhibitory) satisfy $x+a-b<\\theta\\le x+a$, where $\\theta$ is the firing threshold. This demonstrates that the firing outcome depends not just on the total input but on the sequence of arrivals. If the difference between $x+a$ and $\\theta$ is strictly positive (a \"strict margin\"), this response is robust to small perturbations in the target state, aggregate E/I pulse masses, and bounded drift in the system.\n\nWe extend this microscopic effect to a moderately sparse random block graph that is Dale-compatible (meaning each neuron is either purely excitatory or inhibitory, in accordance with Dale's law), with block sizes $q_N$ satisfying $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to0$ as the network size $N$ grows. Both network families share the same graph structure and initial data. Along any deterministic joint scaling $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow0$, the difference in their population-averaged firing counts is $1/2+o_{L^1}(1)$, meaning that, on average, half the population exhibits a different firing response in the two systems, up to a vanishing error. This quantifies the macroscopic impact of arrival order, showing it is not washed out by averaging.\n\nA bounded-degree construction and a later probe confirm that this discrepancy is macroscopic and can persist through the reset mechanism of the neurons. However, when synaptic kernels have fixed positive delays and only finitely many classes, the network can enter a stable regime: before the firing threshold is grazed, typewise-mixing (i.e., sufficiently random mixing between neuron types) sparse networks converge to a delayed class mean-field system, where the effect of arrival order is averaged out. For directed Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi graphs with mean degree $\u03bb_N$ and empirical type distribution $\u03c0_N$, the deviation from the mean-field limit is bounded by $O_P(\u03bb_N^{-1/2}+\\|\u03c0_N-\u03c0\\|_1)$ when $\u03bb_N\\to\\infty$ and $\u03bb_N/N\\to0$, where $\u03c0$ is the limiting type distribution. This result separates the regime of stable averaging at fixed delay from the singular collapse regime, where the delay vanishes and arrival order information is lost.\n\nIn summary, our findings show that componentwise weak convergence of synaptic measures is insufficient to determine the fast-synapse limit in threshold-reset networks: the microscopic order of excitatory and inhibitory arrivals encodes information essential for the firing response, and this information can persist at the macroscopic level in sparse networks. This highlights the importance of considering arrival order in modeling and analyzing such systems, especially in the singular limit of vanishing delays. Limitations of this work include the focus on specific network architectures and event protocols; open questions remain regarding the generality of these effects in broader classes of networks and under different dynamical rules."}}